{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat client_brief.md'","aggregated_output":"# About my business — answers to the questions you would ask me\n\nDon't wait for me, I'm not available this week. Everything I can tell you is\nbelow; where something is missing, make your best assumption and say so.\n\n## Who I am\nI'm Lucía Ferreyra, a potter. My studio is **Taller Lento**, a one-person\nceramics studio in my converted garage in Chacarita, Buenos Aires\n(10 minutes' walk from the Federico Lacroze station). I've been doing it\nfull-time for two years.\n\n## What I sell\n1. **Handmade mugs** (stoneware, my own glazes — the speckled \"yerba\" green is\n   the bestseller). ARS 28,000 each (about US$20). Also small bowls at\n   ARS 22,000 and the occasional set. My kiln and my hands cap me at about\n   60 mugs a month.\n2. **Monthly wheel-throwing classes.** One group: Saturdays 10:00–13:00, four\n   Saturdays a month, max 6 students (I have 6 wheels). ARS 120,000 per person\n   per month (about US$85), clay and firing included. Students keep what they make.\n3. **Taster class** (\"Probá el torno\"): one Saturday afternoon, 2.5 hours,\n   ARS 40,000 (about US$28). I run it when I have enough people asking —\n   maybe once a month.\n\n## Where the money really comes from\nThe mugs are what people see, but the classes are what keep the studio alive.\nA full class group pays more than a whole month of mugs, it's predictable,\nand it doesn't depend on the weather at the fair. Right now the Saturday group\nusually has **3 or 4 of 6 seats filled**. About half of new students stay for\na second month; the ones who stay 3+ months are my best people — they bring\nfriends and buy mugs as gifts. I'd love to open a **second group on Wednesday\nevenings (19:00–22:00)** if I could fill it.\n\nWhen I picture \"it worked\", it's a stranger booking a class — ideally the\nmonthly one, or a taster that turns into the monthly — and then staying.\nA tourist buying one mug is nice but it doesn't change my month.\n\n## How people find me today\n- **Weekend design fair** — Sundays at a craft fair in Palermo (stall costs\n  ARS 35,000 per Sunday). Most mug sales happen here. Maybe 40% of fair buyers\n  are tourists (lots of English speakers, some Brazilians). People pick up the\n  mugs, ask \"do you give classes?\", and I say \"follow me on Instagram\" — and\n  that's mostly where it ends.\n- **Instagram** @taller.lento — 1,900 followers. I post when I remember,\n  maybe once a week: finished pieces, sometimes a Reel of the wheel (those get\n  5–10x more views). Captions in Spanish only.\n- **WhatsApp** — all bookings happen by WhatsApp message. Payment by bank\n  transfer or Mercado Pago link. I keep the student list in my phone contacts.\n- Link in bio goes to a Linktree with my WhatsApp and a PDF price list.\n- No website, no email list, no Google Maps listing.\n- Word of mouth: most current students came from a friend of a student.\n\n## Who buys\n- **Mugs:** tourists at the fair (souvenir, \"made in Buenos Aires\"), and\n  porteñas 25–45 buying gifts.\n- **Classes:** mostly women 28–45 who work in offices or design/tech jobs,\n  living in Chacarita, Colegiales, Villa Crespo, Palermo. They want something\n  off-screen and calm, with their hands. Some come in pairs with a friend.\n  A few expats/digital nomads (English speakers) asked about classes in English\n  — I can teach in English fine.\n\n## Problems I know about\n- Students drop off after the first month, often with \"my schedule changed\".\n- Taster-class people rarely convert to the monthly class — I don't follow up.\n- Fair people ask about classes but I never get their contact.\n- I can't ship internationally (too expensive, breakage), so tourists only buy\n  what they can carry.\n- Sometimes a café asks about wholesale mugs; I don't really want that — the\n  margins are bad and it eats kiln space.\n\n## My limits\n- **Marketing time: about 5 hours a week, total.** The rest is throwing,\n  glazing, firing, teaching and the fair.\n- **Marketing budget: about US$50 a month** (around ARS 70,000), not counting\n  the fair stall.\n- I'm not a marketer. Please write it so I understand it without a\n  dictionary. I'm comfortable with Instagram, WhatsApp and Google Sheets;\n  I don't want to learn complicated tools.\n- My audience is Spanish-speaking locals and English-speaking tourists/expats.\n\n## What I want in 6 months\nSaturday group full (6/6) every month, a Wednesday group running with at\nleast 4 students, and more students staying past their second month.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/valentinakoniukhova/.agents/skills/marketing-ideas/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: marketing-ideas\ndescription: \"When the user needs marketing ideas, inspiration, or strategies for their SaaS or software product. Also use when the user asks for 'marketing ideas,' 'growth ideas,' 'how to market,' 'marketing strategies,' 'marketing tactics,' 'ways to promote,' 'ideas to grow,' 'what else can I try,' 'I don't know how to market this,' 'brainstorm marketing,' or 'what marketing should I do.' Use this as a starting point whenever someone is stuck or looking for inspiration on how to grow. For specific channel execution, see the relevant skill (paid-ads, social-content, email-sequence, etc.).\"\nmetadata:\n  version: 1.1.0\n---\n\n# Marketing Ideas for SaaS\n\nYou are a marketing strategist with a library of 139 proven marketing ideas. Your goal is to help users find the right marketing strategies for their specific situation, stage, and resources.\n\n## How to Use This Skill\n\n**Check for product marketing context first:**\nIf `.agents/product-marketing-context.md` exists (or `.claude/product-marketing-context.md` in older setups), read it before asking questions. Use that context and only ask for information not already covered or specific to this task.\n\nWhen asked for marketing ideas:\n1. Ask about their product, audience, and current stage if not clear\n2. Suggest 3-5 most relevant ideas based on their context\n3. Provide details on implementation for chosen ideas\n4. Consider their resources (time, budget, team size)\n\n---\n\n## Ideas by Category (Quick Reference)\n\n| Category | Ideas | Examples |\n|----------|-------|----------|\n| Content & SEO | 1-10 | Programmatic SEO, Glossary marketing, Content repurposing |\n| Competitor | 11-13 | Comparison pages, Marketing jiu-jitsu |\n| Free Tools | 14-22 | Calculators, Generators, Chrome extensions |\n| Paid Ads | 23-34 | LinkedIn, Google, Retargeting, Podcast ads |\n| Social & Community | 35-44 | LinkedIn audience, Reddit marketing, Short-form video |\n| Email | 45-53 | Founder emails, Onboarding sequences, Win-back |\n| Partnerships | 54-64 | Affiliate programs, Integration marketing, Newsletter swaps |\n| Events | 65-72 | Webinars, Conference speaking, Virtual summits |\n| PR & Media | 73-76 | Press coverage, Documentaries |\n| Launches | 77-86 | Product Hunt, Lifetime deals, Giveaways |\n| Product-Led | 87-96 | Viral loops, Powered-by marketing, Free migrations |\n| Content Formats | 97-109 | Podcasts, Courses, Annual reports, Year wraps |\n| Unconventional | 110-122 | Awards, Challenges, Guerrilla marketing |\n| Platforms | 123-130 | App marketplaces, Review sites, YouTube |\n| International | 131-132 | Expansion, Price localization |\n| Developer | 133-136 | DevRel, Certifications |\n| Audience-Specific | 137-139 | Referrals, Podcast tours, Customer language |\n\n**For the complete list with descriptions**: See [references/ideas-by-category.md](references/ideas-by-category.md)\n\n---\n\n## Implementation Tips\n\n### By Stage\n\n**Pre-launch:**\n- Waitlist referrals (#79)\n- Early access pricing (#81)\n- Product Hunt prep (#78)\n\n**Early stage:**\n- Content & SEO (#1-10)\n- Community (#35)\n- Founder-led sales (#47)\n\n**Growth stage:**\n- Paid acquisition (#23-34)\n- Partnerships (#54-64)\n- Events (#65-72)\n\n**Scale:**\n- Brand campaigns\n- International (#131-132)\n- Media acquisitions (#73)\n\n### By Budget\n\n**Free:**\n- Content & SEO\n- Community building\n- Social media\n- Comment marketing\n\n**Low budget:**\n- Targeted ads\n- Sponsorships\n- Free tools\n\n**Medium budget:**\n- Events\n- Partnerships\n- PR\n\n**High budget:**\n- Acquisitions\n- Conferences\n- Brand campaigns\n\n### By Timeline\n\n**Quick wins:**\n- Ads, email, social posts\n\n**Medium-term:**\n- Content, SEO, community\n\n**Long-term:**\n- Brand, thought leadership, platform effects\n\n---\n\n## Top Ideas by Use Case\n\n### Need Leads Fast\n- Google Ads (#31) - High-intent search\n- LinkedIn Ads (#28) - B2B targeting\n- Engineering as Marketing (#15) - Free tool lead gen\n\n### Building Authority\n- Conference Speaking (#70)\n- Book Marketing (#104)\n- Podcasts (#107)\n\n### Low Budget Growth\n- Easy Keyword Ranking (#1)\n- Reddit Marketing (#38)\n- Comment Marketing (#44)\n\n### Product-Led Growth\n- Viral Loops (#93)\n- Powered By Marketing (#87)\n- In-App Upsells (#91)\n\n### Enterprise Sales\n- Investor Marketing (#133)\n- Expert Networks (#57)\n- Conference Sponsorship (#72)\n\n---\n\n## Output Format\n\nWhen recommending ideas, provide for each:\n\n- **Idea name**: One-line description\n- **Why it fits**: Connection to their situation\n- **How to start**: First 2-3 implementation steps\n- **Expected outcome**: What success looks like\n- **Resources needed**: Time, budget, skills required\n\n---\n\n## Task-Specific Questions\n\n1. What's your current stage and main growth goal?\n2. What's your marketing budget and team size?\n3. What have you already tried that worked or didn't?\n4. What competitor tactics do you admire?\n\n---\n\n## Related Skills\n\n- **programmatic-seo**: For scaling SEO content (#4)\n- **competitor-alternatives**: For comparison pages (#11)\n- **email-sequence**: For email marketing tactics\n- **free-tool-strategy**: For engineering as marketing (#15)\n- **referral-program**: For viral growth (#93)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
